WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:12.480
When I tell you Simon Stålenhag’s The Labyrinth&nbsp;
is a maze you’ll never escape… I don’t think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:18.120
I’m exaggerating. A sci-fi horror artbook&nbsp;
where a mysterious phenomenon turns Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:25.860
into a world of ash and decay, the setup seems&nbsp;
simple. Likewise, the characters — a trio of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:31.740
survivors journeying across the wastes — seem&nbsp;
easy to relate to. Yet the deeper you venture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:37.200
the more you realize The Labyrinth is&nbsp;
a complex tangle of secrets, mysteries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:44.640
and revelations so gripping that it never truly&nbsp;
lets you go. So, for this entry into the archive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.280
we’ll attempt to decipher this masterpiece&nbsp;
of dark worldbuilding. And like my videos on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:56.220
Stålenhag’s other series, you can purchase the&nbsp;
artbook using the links in the description. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:06.420
let’s enter the maze of The Labyrinth… The end of&nbsp;
the world begins with a whisper. Mysterious black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:13.020
globes appear in the sky one day, their arrival so&nbsp;
subtle and quiet it is at first thought to be some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:19.320
sort of natural cosmic phenomenon — as if Earth&nbsp;
simply drifted into an unknown part of the galaxy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:25.980
Yet the spheres seem to move in formation, gliding&nbsp;
through the landscape like they’re following a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:32.460
coordinated plan of action. And soon, planet Earth&nbsp;
begins to wither, the atmosphere growing toxic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:40.140
over a timescale of decades. It is a chillingly&nbsp;
subtle apocalypse, slow and almost imperceptible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:47.220
All attempts to destroy the globes prove futile,&nbsp;
with military crafts left warped into dreamlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:52.920
spirals of matter — as if space itself has&nbsp;
been twisted. Rendered completely inoperable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:59.640
these misshapen weapons lie forgotten in fields&nbsp;
alongside all hope of defeating the spheres.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:05.580
The apocalypse in The Labyrinth is so far beyond&nbsp;
human comprehension that the narrative could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:11.400
be classified as ‘Cosmic Horror’ — a genre&nbsp;
that extracts terror from the unknowable and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:18.420
otherworldly. The black globes cannot be reasoned&nbsp;
with, rationalized, or removed. They are utterly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:25.680
insurmountable: an astronomical nightmare that&nbsp;
humanity has no hope of escaping. “- Each player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:31.200
gets two completely different mazes! -” The&nbsp;
profound helplessness that incomprehensible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.720
phenomena instill can be better understood&nbsp;
in conversation with the film The Endless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:42.120
another work of cosmic horror that follows a&nbsp;
cult which has emerged around a mysterious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:48.780
reality-bending entity. Like the threat in The&nbsp;
Labyrinth, the entity in The Endless is subtle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:55.440
and insidious — only perceptible in the elusive&nbsp;
ways it warps space. Yet the dread it conjures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:01.500
is palpable, felt in scenes where cult members&nbsp;
play tug-of-war with an unseen force in the sky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:08.760
Or when three moons rise overhead — a visual that&nbsp;
like the black globes of The Labyrinth conveys the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:13.740
celestial scale of the threat. “- The first one to&nbsp;
get through the maze with all three balls lights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:20.880
up the tower! -” Over a decade after the&nbsp;
globes’ arrival, the sun — appearing faint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:27.360
and green through an ammonia-choked atmosphere&nbsp;
— rises over a landscape of ash and ruin. Only a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.000
small population of humans avoided the extinction&nbsp;
that befell the vast majority of life on Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:39.480
Yet it is in this era of dust and decay that&nbsp;
the primary narrative of The Labyrinth begins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.960
Three protagonists set out across the desert in a&nbsp;
research vehicle. Two are siblings and employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:52.020
of Kungshall — an underground facility that is one&nbsp;
of the last safe havens for humanity. The third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:58.740
is a child and former refugee the siblings took&nbsp;
in during the crisis years. All three carry with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:06.240
them dark secrets from before civilization’s fall…&nbsp;
The trio journeys toward an uninhabited research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:12.120
outpost, the siblings hoping a break in routine&nbsp;
will help the child, who has been showing signs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:18.240
of acute psychological distress at Kungshall. They&nbsp;
also plan to conduct some light research on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:24.000
changed surface. After all, such expeditions&nbsp;
are common and should be perfectly safe…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.660
Yet there are other things in the otherwise&nbsp;
barren landscape. Things that don’t resemble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:37.740
life on earth… yet seem to be alive all the same.&nbsp;
Answers to what these entities are and why they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:43.800
seem so at home in an otherwise apocalyptic&nbsp;
biosphere can only be found by venturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:51.420
deeper into The Labyrinth. “- Roll the balls and&nbsp;
roll them fast, hurry, hurry, don’t be last. -”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:58.320
After a long drive across the somber mesas, the&nbsp;
trio at last arrives at the isolated outpost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.840
Although half-buried in dunes of ash, the&nbsp;
vacant station represents one of the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:10.140
safe environments in the age of the globes. And&nbsp;
yet while the interior is protected from dust and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:16.800
ruin, the environment doesn’t feel safe. In the&nbsp;
garage, a mural depicts dandelion seeds blowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:23.220
in the wind — a visual so incongruous with the&nbsp;
apocalyptic reality of the planet it becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:30.540
almost eerie. This uncanniness only grows more&nbsp;
intense deeper within the station, where objects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:37.140
remain stacked in neat rows. There’s a sense of&nbsp;
desperation in these scenes: the impossibly clean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:43.320
interiors a way to pretend that nothing beyond&nbsp;
the facility walls has changed. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:50.760
common objects take on a cadaverous quality, stuck&nbsp;
in time like insects trapped in amber. The feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:57.420
of falseness can be so extreme, these interiors&nbsp;
can seem more hostile than the barren desert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:03.300
outside — an environment that, while far from&nbsp;
pleasant, is at the very least straightforward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:10.560
The uncanniness that these altered interiors&nbsp;
exude isn’t easy to put into words. Compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:16.080
your typical sinister locale, nothing about&nbsp;
the research station is overtly menacing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:23.100
And on a cultural level, we all know what a creepy&nbsp;
interior is supposed to look like. There’s usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:28.800
cobwebs and spiders, gothic hallways with&nbsp;
dim lighting and long shadows — the perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:35.760
stage for something to pop out for the inevitable&nbsp;
scare. …But there’s another kind of interior. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:42.000
that lacks every traditional element of a sinister&nbsp;
dwelling, yet can be infinitely more unsettling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:49.260
The Overlook Hotel from The Shining isn’t dark,&nbsp;
or cramped, or run-down — it’s a bright, spacious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:55.920
clean venue. Yes, it’s haunted by the dead, both&nbsp;
metaphorically and perhaps quite literally — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:01.680
as a hotel, it honestly looks nice. Yet&nbsp;
through the eye of director Stanley Kubrick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:07.740
the building feels… unwell. Everything&nbsp;
from the dizzying patterns on the carpet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:13.860
to the queasy colors of the rooms, to the sheer&nbsp;
uncanniness of spaces meant to hold thousands more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:19.800
people than the three main characters, broadcasts&nbsp;
a sense of… wrongness, for lack of a better word.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:26.520
As the leads become increasingly psychologically&nbsp;
distressed, the unfriendly setting feels like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:33.240
reflection of their growing mental unwellness —&nbsp;
and perhaps even the cause. It's fitting, then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:39.000
that the facility in The Labyrinth mirrors The&nbsp;
Overlook Hotel — both in its unnerving aesthetics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:44.940
and disturbed occupants. For like the characters&nbsp;
in The Shining, the three leads in The Labyrinth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:49.860
are haunted by ghosts from their past, pushing&nbsp;
them to the point where they seem genuinely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:55.320
unstable — although for much of the narrative,&nbsp;
it’s difficult to determine which characters are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.320 --> 00:08:01.920
dangerous, and which are in danger. At the end of&nbsp;
The Shining, lead character Jack Torrance reaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:07.620
a point where he tries to hunt down his family&nbsp;
with an axe. It's a looming potential outcome that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:13.140
stays with you as you read through The Labyrinth,&nbsp;
and wonder how dark things will get before you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:21.280
reach the center... “- Dig Dug digs his own mazes.&nbsp;
He digs for balloon-men. He digs for dragons. -”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:28.560
As a suffocating tension builds within the&nbsp;
station, the brother and sister continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:34.800
to make daily expeditions into the surrounding&nbsp;
wasteland to study Earth’s new environment. And in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:41.580
the emaciated remains of what was once a city, new&nbsp;
forms of life have arisen. Amongst the wreckage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:47.880
gargantuan stationary organisms sprout from&nbsp;
the ashen sand like enormous fans of coral.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:53.820
These lifeforms seem at home in the new climate,&nbsp;
as if the globes have terraformed Earth to match&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:59.460
the biosphere of their place of origin. Yet&nbsp;
these coral specimens are just the beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:05.580
Deeper within the maze of the city, tremendous&nbsp;
stalks grow inside the husks of buildings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:11.340
having infested the structures like hermit&nbsp;
crabs occupying a shell. The tops of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:17.460
stalks stretch towards the diffused green light&nbsp;
of the sun, the scene evoking a photograph taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:22.980
far below the waves. Imagery reminiscent of&nbsp;
the deep sea appears throughout The Labyrinth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:28.620
with the suits needed to survive on the&nbsp;
surface evocative of not just astronauts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:35.340
but also old-school divers. There’s a sense of&nbsp;
incompatibility — that the land has become as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:42.480
unsuitable for human life as the deepest oceanic&nbsp;
trench. Elements of cosmic horror once again rear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:48.480
their heads in these scenes, as the siblings are&nbsp;
utterly unable to explain the phenomena around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:54.240
them. In one image that didn’t make it into the&nbsp;
final artbook, a towering squid-like creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:59.760
spreads ribbon-shaped tentacles throughout&nbsp;
the city. There is no understanding this new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:06.840
environment — there is only danger. In this&nbsp;
respect, the wasteland is resonant with The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:13.380
Shimmer from the film Annihilation. A psychedelic&nbsp;
region that an alien entity transforms into a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:19.200
ecosystem, The Shimmer can be deadly for humans&nbsp;
to traverse. Yet like Earth in The Labyrinth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:26.040
The Shimmer isn’t devoid of life — instead, it’s&nbsp;
overflowing with new types of organisms. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:31.020
it’s left ambiguous if the force behind The&nbsp;
Shimmer even comprehends that it’s causing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:37.260
harm — an ambiguity that extends to The Labyrinth.&nbsp;
Although little comfort to the survivors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:42.660
there’s a possibility the near-extinction&nbsp;
of humanity was just an accident — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:48.420
unintentional byproduct of some unrelated&nbsp;
operation. The new world might be toxic to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:54.000
lifeforms adapted to the old one — but clearly&nbsp;
some entities prosper in these new conditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:11:00.000
growing to colossal sizes. Perhaps whatever&nbsp;
intelligence is behind the changed biosphere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:06.840
thought only of the benefit to themselves, and&nbsp;
never factored in the suffering of others. Yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:11.820
the creators of the black globes are not the&nbsp;
only faction in The Labyrinth that made callous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:18.720
calculations to survive. The city is haunted by&nbsp;
organic detritus – but also the wrecks of giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:25.200
armored drones, employed by the military police&nbsp;
during the crisis years. And these destructive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:32.980
weapons weren’t just aimed at the globes... “- And&nbsp;
now he’s digging his way into homes everywhere. -”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:43.800
Later in The Labyrinth, we learn that in the years&nbsp;
leading up to the surface becoming completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:50.400
uninhabitable, panic and desperation spread among&nbsp;
civilians. The air became difficult to breathe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:57.120
and refugees urgently sought shelter from&nbsp;
the sphere’s toxins. Rumors of Kungshall — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:02.820
hermetically sealed city with room for only one&nbsp;
hundred-thousand inhabitants — spread to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:09.120
desperate masses. As a carefully constructed&nbsp;
micro-ecosystem, Kungshall could not hope to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:15.540
shelter the many needy people who sought refuge.&nbsp;
And so, the protectors of the facility — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:21.840
siblings included — made a grim calculation. One&nbsp;
they’d spend the rest of their lives trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:28.380
justify. A massive quarantine zone was erected&nbsp;
around the site, with heavily armed guards and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:34.500
robots patrolling the border. All possible threats&nbsp;
to the project were annihilated. In order to save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:40.440
humanity, the employees of Kungshall believed&nbsp;
they needed to be inhumane for a period of time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:47.520
The strategy was simple: deterrence. Methods of&nbsp;
instilling fear by any means necessary became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:53.760
common practice. There was no official name for&nbsp;
what the guards of Kungshall were doing. No name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.760 --> 00:13:00.840
was needed. And in many respects, the plan was a&nbsp;
resounding success. The streams of people seeking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:06.960
safety diminished. The message had been received:&nbsp;
whatever misery the survivors were fleeing from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:13.620
something much worse awaited them at Kungshall.&nbsp;
Yet cruelty does not vanish. It lingers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:18.420
festering, in the souls of those who wield&nbsp;
it, and in those whom it is wielded against.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:24.480
Among the refugees who suffered at the hands of&nbsp;
Kungshall was the child the siblings would later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:29.820
take in — a decision they made to ease their&nbsp;
guilty consciences and forget the darkness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:37.500
of the past. But what some seek to forget, others&nbsp;
shall always remember… “- They think I’m trapped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:42.000
but I’ll escape.” “Tunnel runner, the new video&nbsp;
game where you don’t look down on the maze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:48.300
you’re in it.” “Monster! -” The people in The&nbsp;
Labyrinth are a tangle of moral complexities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:54.540
knots you turn over and over in your head as&nbsp;
you try to unravel them, struggling to reconcile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:59.640
their many contradictions. The characters&nbsp;
themselves seem lost within their own memories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:06.060
unable to decide the right path forward. This&nbsp;
conflict is represented by a maze that comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:12.720
from a computer game the child plays — which grows&nbsp;
larger and larger as the story reaches its climax…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:20.040
There is a literal maze at the heart of&nbsp;
The Shining as well, an immense expanse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:25.740
of hedges that dominate the hotel grounds. For&nbsp;
a film known for its puzzle-like storytelling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:30.540
it seems like a logical inclusion.&nbsp;
Yet as is the case in The Labyrinth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:36.120
the maze in The Shining is also symbolic of the&nbsp;
psyche of the main characters — in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:41.580
Jack Torrance and his descent into&nbsp;
utter madness. Perhaps the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:47.400
mystery of The Shining is the source of Jack’s&nbsp;
insanity. Is it something he brought with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:53.220
as the film implies he abused his spouse and&nbsp;
child in the past? Is it symptomatic of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:59.220
hotel itself, a seemingly hostile locale?&nbsp;
Or is it something beyond comprehension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:06.000
the scheme of an otherworldly entity we can’t hope&nbsp;
to understand? This plot point acts like a prism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:12.300
with the refracted truth appearing differently&nbsp;
to each member of the audience. The ending of The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:18.480
Labyrinth is just as multi-faceted — in fact,&nbsp;
it might be more so. Because in The Shining,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:23.460
out of the three lead characters, there’s at&nbsp;
least a distinct villain in the form of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.460 --> 00:15:30.000
axe-wielding Jack Torrance. But The Labyrinth has&nbsp;
no villain, not really — just people who had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:35.520
make terrible decisions and people who can’t let&nbsp;
go. And while you hope they’ll be able to escape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:41.100
to find a way out of the maze they’ve all trapped&nbsp;
themselves in, there’s no clear exit in sight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:48.120
Famously, in the final scenes of The Shining,&nbsp;
Torrance becomes lost within the very labyrinth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:54.720
that represents his winding path towards insanity.&nbsp;
The film ends not with the red-hot swing of an axe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.720 --> 00:16:00.900
but with a long, slow fade-out as he succumbs&nbsp;
to the cold of a snowstorm — the blizzard an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:06.780
apparent reminder that there are more powerful&nbsp;
and elemental forces at work in these stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:14.580
than humans. The Labyrinth ends with a flood.&nbsp;
The ammonia in the atmosphere falls like rain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:20.880
and dissolves the water frozen under the ash —&nbsp;
a final, unexpected stage of the changing Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:27.780
The dark liquid wells up in the interior of the&nbsp;
station, drowning the red corridors. And in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:33.720
midst of this catastrophe, the pressure building&nbsp;
within the lead characters finally bursts in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:40.380
way I found both shocking and somehow completely&nbsp;
inevitable considering all that came before. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:45.780
I won’t give away all the final twists and turns&nbsp;
to The Labyrinth, if you’d like to know everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:52.080
that happens, you can check out the book for&nbsp;
yourself… “- Better check the map. It’s the exit!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.080 --> 00:17:01.080
To another maze… -” The Labyrinth doesn’t exist&nbsp;
in a vacuum. Simon Stålenhag’s other works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:05.940
Tales from the Loop and The Electric State,&nbsp;
explore related themes of bureaucratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:12.120
inhumanity and lingering trauma through a lens of&nbsp;
visually-stunning sci-fi. Yet though both of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:18.060
aforementioned works have their serious elements,&nbsp;
I believe The Labyrinth is ultimately Stålenhag’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:24.420
most solemn story yet — one I still feel a part of&nbsp;
me is trapped within. While all narratives have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:29.910
come to an end, the mysteries and tragedies of&nbsp;
The Labyrinth lingered long after the final page&nbsp;&nbsp;

